# openbox-autostart — X11 FALLBACK kiosk autostart.
#
# Install destination: ~darwin/.config/openbox/autostart
# Used only when cage (Wayland) is unavailable on the image; apply-appliance.sh
# chooses cage (darwin-kiosk.service) vs openbox and installs exactly one path.
#
# This file is sourced by openbox-session at X11 session start. It disables the
# screensaver / DPMS, then execs the shared kiosk entry point so the X11 and
# Wayland paths converge on identical cockpit logic.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SIBLING NOTE — openbox also needs autologin + startx.
# Openbox does not start X11 by itself; the autologged-in `darwin` shell must
# launch it. Add this one-liner to ~darwin/.bash_profile so logging in on tty1
# brings up X (and therefore this autostart). apply-appliance.sh writes it:
#
#   [ -z "${DISPLAY:-}" ] && [ "$(tty)" = "/dev/tty1" ] && exec startx
#
# Pair that with a getty autologin drop-in for `darwin` on tty1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Best-effort screen-blank / power-management off (guarded; never abort).
# The kiosk entry point also disables blanking, so this is belt-and-suspenders.
if command -v xset >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  xset s off || true       # disable screensaver
  xset -dpms || true       # disable DPMS power management
  xset s noblank || true   # do not blank the video on screensaver timeout
fi

# Hand off to the shared kiosk entry point. exec so openbox supervises the
# launcher directly and a kiosk exit tears down the session cleanly for restart.
exec /usr/local/bin/darwin-kiosk.sh
